-- the reason code's own text (§18): the payload's header, how-to-read, glossary and supplementary header
- Merchandise: goods the cardholder buys, usually physical.
- A service: anything else the merchant provides. For example: travel and transport; accommodation and holidays; event and attraction entry; memberships and subscriptions; lessons and courses; gaming and betting accounts; repairs and appointments. Access over time, such as an account or a subscription, is a service, and so is food and drink served to be eaten or drunk on the premises. Whether a one-off digital download is merchandise or a service is not settled.
- A future service: a service paid for at purchase and due on a later date. It remains a future service once that date has passed.
- An agent: a merchant, such as a travel or ticket agency, that arranges a service another business provides.
- One purchase can include merchandise and services. Each argument answers only its own part: proof the goods arrived does not answer a complaint about the service.', 'Supplementary arguments — preconditions. These show the dispute should not have been raised under this reason code at all. They supplement the arguments above: nominate one whenever the records show it, whether or not an argument above fits, and list only those you nominate. The time-limit arguments and early filing depend on the time-limits section: rely on the results shown there and never count days yourself.', ARRAY['4722','7922']::text[], ARRAY['7922']::text[], 'Never mention how the payment was authenticated, 3-D Secure, terms and conditions or refund policies', ARRAY['3-D Secure','3DS','authenticat','terms and conditions','T&C','refund policy','friendly fraud','first-party fraud','fraudster','scam','dishonest']::text[])
ON CONFLICT (network, code) DO UPDATE SET name = EXCLUDED.name, rulebook_edition = EXCLUDED.rulebook_edition, catalogue_version = EXCLUDED.catalogue_version, grounds_header = EXCLUDED.grounds_header, how_to_read = EXCLUDED.how_to_read, terms_used = EXCLUDED.terms_used, supplementary_header = EXCLUDED.supplementary_header, agency_mccs = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs, agency_mccs_reviewed = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs_reviewed, never_mention = EXCLUDED.never_mention, banned_phrases = EXCLUDED.banned_phrases;
- Merchandise: goods the cardholder buys, usually physical.
- A service: anything else the merchant provides. For example: accommodation and holidays; travel and transport; vehicle and equipment rental; event and attraction entry; memberships and subscriptions; lessons and courses. Access over time, such as an account or a subscription, is a service. Whether a one-off digital download is merchandise or a service is not settled.
- Cancelled: the cardholder told the merchant they no longer wanted the merchandise or service, by any route, whether or not the merchant agreed.
- Returned, or sent back: the cardholder sent the merchandise back to the merchant.
- The terms, or the policy: the merchant''s return, refund or cancellation rules for this purchase. A limited policy restricts returns or cancellations: for example non-refundable, a deadline or a fee for cancelling, or store credit only.
- A reservation: accommodation, merchandise or a service, such as a rental, booked with the merchant and held for the cardholder as agreed, where the cardholder gave card details but paid nothing when booking. A booking paid for in advance is not a reservation in this sense.
- A No-Show charge: a charge made because the cardholder neither cancelled a reservation nor claimed it.
- A timeshare: a purchase from a merchant that sells, markets, manages or rents holiday property and related products — traditional timeshares, and also holiday or vacation clubs, travel clubs and holiday homes.
- A sale made at a distance or away from the merchant''s premises: a sale made online, by phone or post, or somewhere other than the merchant''s own premises, such as the cardholder''s home.
- In Europe: in the rulebook''s Europe Region, which includes the European Union, the United Kingdom, Norway, Switzerland, Israel and Türkiye among others.
- The unused part: the part of a service paid for but not used before cancelling, such as nights not stayed or sessions not attended.
- A refund, credit or reversal: money the merchant sent back to the card for this purchase. A reversal cancels the payment itself; a refund or credit is a new payment back. A credit receipt is the merchant''s record of a refund to the card.
- A travel and entertainment purchase: a purchase from an airline, a cruise line, a hotel or other lodging merchant, a travel agency or a vehicle rental merchant, or, in the United States, a passenger railway. Event and attraction tickets are not in this group.
- VAT: value-added tax.
- The merchant category code: the code on the payment that says what kind of business the merchant is.
- The payment record: the transaction as the payment system recorded it, shown in the case. Item-level detail means the record lists what was bought, item by item.
- The issuer''s filing: the dispute as the issuer raised it, with its questionnaire and any documents it attached.
- One purchase can include merchandise and services. Each argument answers only its own part.', 'Supplementary arguments — preconditions. These show the dispute should not have been raised under this reason code at all, or not for the amount claimed. They supplement the arguments above: nominate one whenever the records show it, whether or not an argument above fits, and list only those you nominate. The time-limit arguments and the record checks depend on their own sections: rely on the results shown there, and never count days or work out a result yourself.', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], 'Never mention how the payment was authenticated or 3-D Secure', ARRAY['3-D Secure','3DS','authenticat','terms and conditions','T&C','refund policy','friendly fraud','first-party fraud','fraudster','scam','dishonest']::text[])
ON CONFLICT (network, code) DO UPDATE SET name = EXCLUDED.name, rulebook_edition = EXCLUDED.rulebook_edition, catalogue_version = EXCLUDED.catalogue_version, grounds_header = EXCLUDED.grounds_header, how_to_read = EXCLUDED.how_to_read, terms_used = EXCLUDED.terms_used, supplementary_header = EXCLUDED.supplementary_header, agency_mccs = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs, agency_mccs_reviewed = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs_reviewed, never_mention = EXCLUDED.never_mention, banned_phrases = EXCLUDED.banned_phrases;
- The amount processed, or the amount charged: the amount the transaction record shows was charged to the card for this transaction.
- The amount disputed: the amount the issuer''s dispute claims back, shown in the case.
- The correct amount: the amount the cardholder says should have been charged.
- The difference between the amounts: the part of the amount processed that is said to be wrong — the amount processed less the correct amount. The arguments ask you to find the amounts in the records, never to work out the difference.
- A receipt: the merchant''s record of the sale given to the cardholder, such as a till or terminal receipt, a signed bill, an invoice, an order confirmation or a checkout page. Its total is the amount of everything sold at that time, with taxes, fees and any adjustments.
- The printed amount and the handwritten amount: on a printed receipt or bill, the amount the till printed, and an amount written on it by hand, such as a tip or a total. Where the two differ, the handwritten amount is the one that counts.
- A tip: an amount the cardholder adds for service, often written on the receipt by hand.
- Added up wrongly: a total that does not equal the sum of its items. Digits swapped: a total entered with two digits in the wrong order, such as 19.50 charged for 15.90.
- A refund, credit or reversal: money sent back to the card for the disputed charge. A reversal cancels the payment itself; a refund or credit is a new payment back. A correction is the charge reversed, in whole or in part, so that the right amount stands. A voucher or store credit is not a refund to the card.
- A travel and entertainment merchant: an airline, a cruise line, a hotel or other lodging merchant, a travel agency, a vehicle rental merchant or, in the United States, a passenger railway. Restaurants, shops, and event and attraction ticket sellers are not in this group.
- The price quoted: the price, rate or estimate the merchant gave the cardholder before or when the service began — at booking, check-in or the start of a rental. The final bill, or folio, shows the charges actually made.
- A reservation held with the card: accommodation, a vehicle or another service booked and held for the cardholder, guaranteed with their card details. A no-show charge is a charge made because the cardholder neither cancelled the reservation nor claimed it.
- An advance payment: a deposit or payment for part or all of the price of goods or services that will be provided later. A prepayment is an advance payment of the full price.
- Custom-made goods or services: goods or services made or arranged to the cardholder''s own order.
- An amended amount or a delayed charge: an amount a rental, lodging, campground or cruise merchant changes or adds after check-out, return or disembarkation, for the rental, stay or cruise the cardholder took part in — for example fuel, mileage, tolls, parking or traffic fines, minibar, insurance or damage.
- Merchants that may change a charge after the transaction: merchants that rent vehicles, motor homes, motorcycles, bicycles, boats, aircraft, equipment, tools, furniture, appliances or clothing; hotels and other lodging merchants; campgrounds; and cruise lines.
- An alternative repair estimate: a quote for repairing damage that the cardholder obtains and sends to the merchant after receiving the merchant''s damage documentation.
- An adjustment at an ATM: a correction an ATM operator makes to a deposit or cash withdrawal at an ATM. It does not arise at an ordinary merchant.
- A different reason code: the dispute belongs under another kind of dispute — goods or services not received or not as described, or a cancellation or refund not given (consumer-dispute and cancellation reason codes); a charge processed twice (the duplicate-processing reason code); a charge in the wrong currency (the incorrect-currency reason code); a charge the cardholder does not recognise (a fraud reason code).
- The issuer''s filing: the dispute as the issuer raised it, with its questionnaire and any documents it attached. The issuer''s documents are those attachments.
- The transaction record: the payment as the payment system recorded it, shown in the case, including the amount processed, the merchant category code and whether the card was present.
- The dispute was raised: the date the issuer processed the dispute, shown in the case.', 'Supplementary arguments — preconditions. These show the dispute should not have been raised under this reason code at all, or not for the amount claimed. They supplement the arguments above: nominate one whenever the records show it, whether or not an argument above fits, and list only those you nominate. The time-limit arguments and the record checks depend on their own sections: rely on the results shown there, and never count days or work out a result yourself.', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], 'Never mention how the payment was authenticated, 3-D Secure, terms and conditions or refund policies', ARRAY['3-D Secure','3DS','authenticat','terms and conditions','T&C','refund policy','friendly fraud','first-party fraud','fraudster','scam','dishonest']::text[])
ON CONFLICT (network, code) DO UPDATE SET name = EXCLUDED.name, rulebook_edition = EXCLUDED.rulebook_edition, catalogue_version = EXCLUDED.catalogue_version, grounds_header = EXCLUDED.grounds_header, how_to_read = EXCLUDED.how_to_read, terms_used = EXCLUDED.terms_used, supplementary_header = EXCLUDED.supplementary_header, agency_mccs = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs, agency_mccs_reviewed = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs_reviewed, never_mention = EXCLUDED.never_mention, banned_phrases = EXCLUDED.banned_phrases;
- Merchandise: goods the cardholder buys, usually physical, including goods delivered on a schedule, such as a subscription box.
- A service: anything else the merchant provides, such as streaming, software, memberships, utilities, lessons, and access to an account or a platform over time.
- A card held on file, or a stored card: card details the cardholder let the merchant keep so that it can take future payments.
- A recurring charge: one of a series of charges the merchant takes from a card held on file at regular intervals, such as monthly or yearly, for goods or services provided at regular intervals under the cardholder''s standing agreement. Subscriptions and memberships are paid this way.
- An installment payment: one of a series of charges for a single purchase, or for the repayment of a single debt, agreed when the purchase was made and spread over a fixed period.
- An unscheduled charge to a card held on file: a charge the merchant takes from a stored card not on a set date but when an event the cardholder agreed to occurs, such as a balance falling below a set level.
- A payment the cardholder made themselves, or initiated by the cardholder: a payment the cardholder started at the time, such as paying at checkout, confirming a renewal or paying in person, as opposed to the merchant charging a card held on file under a standing agreement. The first payment made when signing up is one.
- The sign-up: the cardholder''s original agreement to the subscription or service. A free trial or introductory offer is a period at no charge or a reduced charge before the regular charges begin.
- Withdrawing permission: the cardholder telling the merchant to stop charging the card, by cancelling the subscription or the agreement to be charged.
- The cancellation date: the last date the cardholder is allowed to use the service. It can be later than the date permission was withdrawn: a cardholder who cancels a monthly subscription part-way through a month and is told they can use it until the month ends has the last day of that month as the cancellation date.
- The period a charge pays for: the stretch of service, such as a month or a year, that one recurring charge pays for. A charge taken in advance pays for the coming period; a charge taken after the service, or billed in arrears, pays for the period just ended.
- The unused part, or unused portion: the part of the service a charge paid for that was not used, or merchandise it paid for that was not received.
- Used the service: the cardholder''s own account in use, such as signing in, streaming, ordering, attending or receiving a delivery, shown by a record the service itself made at the time.
- A closed account: the cardholder''s card account or payment credential closed by the issuer. Facilities withdrawn: the issuer taking away the cardholder''s use of the card.
- A card-updating service: a service through which a merchant receives changes to stored card details, including notice that an account was closed.
- A refund, credit or reversal: money the merchant sent back to the card for the disputed charge. A reversal cancels the payment itself; a refund or credit is a new payment back. A voucher or an account credit with the merchant is not a refund to the card.
- In Europe: a transaction made within the rulebook''s Europe Region, on a card issued there at a merchant located there. The Europe Region includes the European Union, the United Kingdom, Norway, Iceland, Switzerland, Israel and Türkiye among others; some European countries, such as Albania, Moldova, Serbia and Ukraine, are in a different region.
- The issuer''s filing: the dispute as the issuer raised it, with its questionnaire and any documents it attached.
- The transaction record: the payment as the payment system recorded it, shown in the case, including whether the card was present and how its details were entered.
- The dispute was raised: the date the issuer processed the dispute, shown in the case.
- One purchase can include merchandise and services. Each argument answers only its own part.', 'Supplementary arguments — preconditions. These show the dispute should not have been raised under this reason code at all, or not for the amount claimed. They supplement the arguments above: nominate one whenever the records show it, whether or not an argument above fits, and list only those you nominate. The time-limit arguments and the record checks depend on their own sections: rely on the results shown there, and never count days or work out a result yourself.', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], 'Never mention how the payment was authenticated or 3-D Secure', ARRAY['3-D Secure','3DS','authenticat','terms and conditions','T&C','refund policy','friendly fraud','first-party fraud','fraudster','scam','dishonest']::text[])
ON CONFLICT (network, code) DO UPDATE SET name = EXCLUDED.name, rulebook_edition = EXCLUDED.rulebook_edition, catalogue_version = EXCLUDED.catalogue_version, grounds_header = EXCLUDED.grounds_header, how_to_read = EXCLUDED.how_to_read, terms_used = EXCLUDED.terms_used, supplementary_header = EXCLUDED.supplementary_header, agency_mccs = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs, agency_mccs_reviewed = EXCLUDED.agency_mccs_reviewed, never_mention = EXCLUDED.never_mention, banned_phrases = EXCLUDED.banned_phrases;
- Merchandise: goods the cardholder buys, usually physical.
- A service: anything else the merchant provides. For example: a stay, a repair, a treatment, an installation, a course, travel or a rental. A service already provided is one the merchant has finished providing, so it can no longer be cancelled. Whether a one-off digital download is merchandise or a service is not settled.
- What was described: what the cardholder was told they would get at the time of purchase — on the receipt, the listing or product page, the order confirmation, the contract or the specification, or what the merchant told them.
- Damaged or defective: broken, faulty, or not working as it should when received.
- Quality: how good the merchandise or service is. A quality complaint says it was poor, not that it was different from what was described.
- The price: what the cardholder was charged for the purchase, as against what they expected, were quoted, saw advertised or found elsewhere.
- Sent back, or returned: the cardholder sent the merchandise back to the merchant. An attempted return: the cardholder tried to send it back and the merchant refused the return, refused a return label, told them not to send it, did not respond, no longer exists, or gave no clear instructions on how to return. A return label: a label or authorisation the merchant gives for sending goods back.
- Cancelled: the cardholder told the merchant they no longer wanted a service, by any route.
- A refund, credit or reversal: money the merchant sent back to the card for this purchase. A reversal cancels the payment itself; a refund or credit is a new payment back. A replacement, repair, exchange, voucher or store credit is not a refund.
- A credit request: the cardholder asking the merchant for their money back, in whole or in part.
- An attempt to resolve: the cardholder contacting the merchant, or someone acting for it, about the problem before the dispute, by any route.
- A liquidator: the person appointed to wind up a merchant that has become insolvent.
- Customs: the government agency that holds goods crossing a border until they are cleared.
- VAT: value-added tax.
- An eating place: a restaurant, café, takeaway or other business that prepares food to eat, whether eaten there, collected or delivered.
- Crypto, or non-fiat currency: a digital currency such as a cryptocurrency, as opposed to a government-issued currency. An NFT: a non-fungible token, a unique digital asset.
- A ramp provider: a business that converts card payments into crypto, or back, either selling crypto to cardholders itself or providing that service for other businesses, such as retailers, crypto exchanges or NFT marketplaces (the business it processes for).
- A travel agency''s commercial virtual card: a business card account with no physical card that a travel agency uses to pay merchants such as hotels, airlines, cruise lines and car rental companies. The contract is the agreement between the travel agency and the merchant setting the terms of the services.
- The issuer''s filing: the dispute as the issuer raised it, with its questionnaire (the issuer''s form) and any documents it attached. The cardholder''s own words: what the cardholder wrote, in the filing or in messages to the merchant.
- The merchant''s complete contact history: every message and contact record the merchant holds with the cardholder for this order, with dates. Its returns history: its records of return requests, labels issued and goods received back.
- The payment record: the transaction as the payment system recorded it, shown in the case.
- The merchant category code: the code on the payment that says what kind of business the merchant is.
- One purchase can include several items, or merchandise and services. Each argument answers only its own part.', 'Supplementary arguments — preconditions. These show the dispute should not have been raised under this reason code at all, or not for the amount claimed. They supplement the arguments above: nominate one whenever the records show it, whether or not an argument above fits, and list only those you nominate. The time-limit arguments and the record checks depend on their own sections: rely on the results shown there, and never count days or work out a result yourself.', '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], 'Never mention how the payment was authenticated or 3-D Secure', ARRAY['3-D Secure','3DS','authenticat','terms and conditions','T&C','refund policy','friendly fraud','first-party fraud','fraudster','scam','dishonest']::text[])
